<Title>: A/A control for <the A/B it calibrates>. DECISION, written before its first run.

Copy this file to <run-dir>/DECISION and replace every <...> before the first run. Both
arms are one binary: <probe>, sha256 <...> (PROVENANCE). It runs exactly as the A/B it
calibrates (the same wl/<workload>.args, quiet() and nice), in the same session, rounds
alternating as the A/B's do. It is diagnostic: it never re-decides the A/B, it says only
whether the A/B's mean gate can inform on this host.

experiment = aa
rounds = 1-6
workloads = w1

quiet.load_max = <as the A/B>
quiet.load_wait_s = <as the A/B>
quiet.compiler_wait_s = <as the A/B>

Reported: per workload, the geomean of the per-round mean ratio and of the p50 ratio, each
round's ratio, acks over 10 ms per run, and the spread: exp(|mean(log r)| + 2 * SE), with
r each round's ratio and SE the standard error of the mean of the per-round log(r). Run it
in the same session as the A/B, interleaved, with at least half the A/B's round count. With the line below, decide.py also says whether the spread is
within it; the A/B's DECISION holds the limit that binds.
aa.spread_max = 1.025
